    id: "8280e102f981bc9812ae",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    categories: ["geopolitica"],
    title: "China's Xi praises 'turnaround' in relationship with Australia",
    summary: "The leaders of Australia and China have held talks on sidelines of APEC summit. Australian PM Anthony Albanese is also slated to meet Chinese Premier Li Qiang.",
    preview: "November 20, 2026 The leaders of Australia and China have held talks on sidelines of APEC summit. Australian PM Anthony Albanese is also slated to meet Chinese Premier Li Qiang. Australian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese and Chinese President Xi Jinping held talks on Friday, following which Xi hailed a \"comprehensive turnaround\" in the ties between two nations and Albanese said his country was engaging \"constructively\" with Beijing. The leaders met on the sidelines of the Asia-Pacific Economic Forum (APEC) summit in China's Shenzhen.",
    body: "November 20, 2026\n\nThe leaders of Australia and China have held talks on sidelines of APEC summit. Australian PM Anthony Albanese is also slated to meet Chinese Premier Li Qiang.\n\nAustralian Prime Minister Anthony Albanese and Chinese President Xi Jinping held talks on Friday, following which Xi hailed a \"comprehensive turnaround\" in the ties between two nations and Albanese said his country was engaging \"constructively\" with Beijing.\n\nThe leaders met on the sidelines of the Asia-Pacific Economic Forum (APEC) summit in China's Shenzhen.\n\nWhat did Xi say to Albanese?\n\nXi pointed out to Albanese that they had met four times since the latter became Australia's Prime Minister in 2022.\n\n\"I think it is fair to say that together we have brought about a comprehensive turnaround in China-Australia relations,\" the Chinese President said.\n\nXi also called for enhancing cooperation in agriculture, energy, the green economy and artificial intelligence, Chinese state news agency Xinhua reported.\n\nWhy is China important for Australia?\n\nChina is Australia's top trading partner.\n\nThe relations between the two countries hit rock bottom under Canberra's former centre-right coalition government, which had called for an independent inquiry into the origins of the coronavirus.\n\nAn angry China retaliated by imposing import curbs worth $13 billion (€12.2 billion) on Australian timber, coal, barley, lobsters and wine.\n\nSince Albanese's centre-left Labor party came to power, China has lifted most of its blocks on Australian exports.\n\nBut friction in ties has surfaced occasionally on issues such as China conducting a missile test in the South Pacific and Australia tightening a foreign investment screening in the critical minerals sector.\n\n\"It is important that Australia continue to engage in a constructive way with China, manage our differences, but not be defined by them,\" Albanese told the media on Friday.\n\n\"What happens in China does matter for Australia,\" he added.\n\nIron ore, a pressure point\n\nAlbanese is expected to travel to Beijing and meet China's Premier Li Qiang later on Friday.\n\nOn Monday, Australia, which is the world's biggest producer of iron ore, called out China's \"manipulation\" of trade in the commodity over Beijing's formation of a central buying body to purchase ore on behalf of its steelmakers.\n\nA single buyer has increased bargaining power in negotiations with sellers.\n\nAlbanese and Li will attend a business roundtable comprising of senior executives from Australian mining giants Rio Tinto and BHP and Chinese company executives.",
    content_status: "full",
    source: "Deutsche Welle",
    url: "https://www.dw.com/en/china-s-xi-praises-turnaround-in-relationship-with-australia/a-79999229?maca=en-rss-en-world-4025-rdf",
