    id: "288e765f3c55699fc0b4",
    cat: "conflitti",
    categories: ["conflitti"],
    title: "Three Kyiv bridges hit during major Russian missile attack",
    summary: "Five people were killed in a wave of strikes which follow Ukraine's successful targeting of the Crimean Bridge.",
    preview: "Russia targeted bridges in Kyiv and railway stations overnight in a major attack launched days after Ukraine badly damaged the strategically important Crimean Bridge. Ballistic missiles hit three bridges in the capital during a nationwide assault which Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky said was \"one of the most massive strikes\" of the war so far and left at least eight dead. Russian President Vladimir Putin had threatened to retaliate for the attack on the Crimean Bridge, which the Kremlin built to link Russia and the occupied peninsula.",
    body: "Russia targeted bridges in Kyiv and railway stations overnight in a major attack launched days after Ukraine badly damaged the strategically important Crimean Bridge.\n\nBallistic missiles hit three bridges in the capital during a nationwide assault which Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky said was \"one of the most massive strikes\" of the war so far and left at least eight dead.\n\nRussian President Vladimir Putin had threatened to retaliate for the attack on the Crimean Bridge, which the Kremlin built to link Russia and the occupied peninsula.\n\nThe latest Russian strikes coincided with a flurry of talks around the war, and as the Kremlin pushed back against a US claim that Russia could be open to ongoing Western-backed security guarantees for Ukraine.\n\nThe three Kyiv bridges targeted in Kyiv overnight - the Podilsko-Voskresensky Bridge, the Darnytskyi Bridge and the Southern Bridge - have been closed to traffic, adding to disruption created by retaliatory strikes on bridges earlier this week.\n\nKyiv Mayor Vitali Klitschko urged people to \"avoid travelling into the city by private car unless absolutely necessary\".\n\nHe added that an unspecified critical infrastructure facility was also damaged and seven medical personnel were injured during the night's assault.\n\nUkraine's major operation to take out the road section of the Crimean Bridge earlier this week was a tactical and symbolic blow for Moscow.\n\nSince illegally annexing Crimea in 2014, Moscow has housed troops and military equipment there, with occupying forces travelling through the peninsula towards the front line.\n\nUkrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky said an operation to weaken the bridge's defences and ultimately cause part of it to collapse had been weeks in the making.\n\nRussian President Vladimir Putin - who personally opened the bridge in 2019 to international condemnation - said the Kremlin's retaliation for the strike would be \"far more painful\".\n\nAir raid sirens lasted non-stop throughout the night and were ongoing into the morning.\n\nUkraine's air force says almost 100 missiles were launched by Russia in its overnight attack - one of the largest attacks since the invasion began in 2022. It also involved around 400 drones.\n\nThe attack was \"one of the most powerful\" yet, according to Yuriy Ignat, head of communications for the Ukrainian air force.\n\nPrime Minister Serhiy Koretsky said 28 people were injured, with an ambulance hospital in Kharkiv among the targets of an attack which hit nine regions.\n\nTwo people were killed in a hit on the Sknyliv railway station in the south-west of Lviv, the city's Mayor Andriy Sadovyi said.\n\nHe shared a picture of the ruined remains of what he said was the station's ticket terminal.\n\nTen people were injured and a rescue operation at the site is ongoing.\n\nA Russian cruise missile landed in Poland around 50km (31 miles) from its border with Ukraine, the Polish Defence Minister Wladyslaw Kosiniak-Kamysz said on Friday.\n\nTwo more unidentified projectiles, suspected to be a drone and a second missile, landed in villages nearby and no one was harmed, he added.\n\nPolish and Swedish jets were scrambled on Nato's eastern flank as Russia's attack on western Ukraine continued. They have since been stood down.\n\nThe attack began hours after European leaders including UK Prime Minister Andy Burnham, French President Emmanuel Macron and German Chancellor Friedrich Merz said they were ready for negotiations involving Moscow to end the war in Ukraine.\n\nThe Berlin meeting of the so-called coalition of the willing, an international alliance of Ukraine's allies committed to providing security support in the event of a peace deal, was attended by Zelensky and US President Donald Trump's envoys.\n\nJared Kushner said the US \"wants to make a deal with Europe by his side\" after fears of an agreement negotiated between the US and Russia were reignited by Trump's one-to-one talks with Putin earlier this week.\n\nHe claimed Russia was \"comfortable\" with Ukraine having security guarantees based on Nato's Article Five, which requires allies to come to a country's defence if it comes under attack.\n\nOn Monday, Russia's deputy foreign minister Sergei Ryabkov pushed back against that, saying \"quasi-Nato\" membership for Ukraine would be \"unacceptable\" to Russia. The Kremlin has long cited the prospect of Ukraine joining Nato as a key justification for its war effort.\n\nThe US president dialled into the Berlin talks from Air Force One on his way back from China, where he met his Russian counterpart and Chinese leader Xi Jinping.\n\nHe reportedly questioned the decision of European leaders to commit â¬70bn (Â£58.6bn; $76.bn) in military funding for Ukraine.",
