    id: "4517641d353957a37561",
    cat: "conflitti",
    categories: ["conflitti"],
    title: "Europe ready for talks with Russia on ending war in Ukraine, leaders say",
    summary: "European allies of Ukraine say they have a \"united\" negotiating position at a summit in Berlin.",
    preview: "European leaders have said they are ready for negotiations with Russia on ending the war in Ukraine, in a strong show of support for Kyiv. \"Europe has a clear and consistent and united negotiating position,\" UK Prime Minister Andy Burnham said, while German Chancellor Friedrich Merz stressed that Moscow's \"maximalist demands are not realistic\". They were speaking at Berlin's summit of Ukraine's allies known as the Coalition of the Willing.",
    body: "European leaders have said they are ready for negotiations with Russia on ending the war in Ukraine, in a strong show of support for Kyiv.\n\n\"Europe has a clear and consistent and united negotiating position,\" UK Prime Minister Andy Burnham said, while German Chancellor Friedrich Merz stressed that Moscow's \"maximalist demands are not realistic\".\n\nThey were speaking at Berlin's summit of Ukraine's allies known as the Coalition of the Willing.\n\nDonald Trump's son-in-law Jared Kushner, one of the two US envoys who were in Berlin, said the US president wanted to make a deal with Moscow \"with Europe by his side\". Russia's Vladimir Putin has so far rejected any European role in the US-led peace talks.\n\nTrump had earlier held historic trilateral talks in Shenzhen, south-eastern China, with Putin and Chinese President Xi Jinping and later dialled into the Berlin summit from Air Force One.\n\nIn a lengthy press conference following his talks with Trump and Xi, Putin addressed the question of multilateral peace talks, saying the Americans were \"insistently\" asking Russia to allow the Europeans to take part.\n\nPutin claimed Russia was not against the idea but said Moscow wanted to clarify \"what Europe will bring to these talks\".\n\n\"That is why, through diplomatic channels, we have invited the Europeans to come to Moscow in order to determine their positions. And after that, we will decide in what format we will continue the dialogue with Ukraine.\"\n\nBut Putin also said that \"every day\" Russia heard that European leaders were talking about war coming in 2030. \"But we are not planning to attack anyone... Is that normal? I do not think so,\" he said. Still, Putin added, Russia understood that \"discussing issues linked to security in Europe is pointless without the Europeans\".\n\nJared Kushner told reporters that the US president had come away from Shenzhen believing that \"peace is achievable\".\n\nStriking an upbeat note, Kushner said that the Russians were willing to trust the instinct of the US envoys \"on our behalf\" that the Europeans should be at the table and that Moscow was now \"comfortable\" with Ukraine having security guarantees based on the idea enshrined in Nato's Article Five that requires allies to come to a country's defence if it comes under attack.\n\nUkrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky, who also was at the summit in Germany's capital, has long sought a guarantee equivalent to Nato's collective defence clause, and there has been little indication that Putin would accept that kind of condition.\n\nRussia rejects outright the idea of Ukrainian membership of Nato, but reports say US negotiators have mulled the idea of giving Kyiv Article Five-style guarantees without being part of the Western defensive alliance if it agrees to painful territorial concessions.\n\nZelensky and the three European leaders â Merz, Burnham and French President Emmanuel Macron â were not as upbeat as Kushner, and the Ukrainian president made clear that the Russian president had spurned every proposal so far: \"Putin has always said no.\"\n\nThe German chancellor was equally sceptical, although he said with US involvement the chance of peace was increasing. Europe was willing to enter \"serious discussions on European security\" with Russia, he explained, \"as long as it's matched with a serious discussion and only first a peace agreement with Ukraine\".\n\n\"These talks have to begin, and we will accept if conditions are acceptable for Europe and Ukraine,\" said Merz.\n\nHe also said any attempts to divide Europe into spheres of influence run by major powers was unacceptable, amid concerns on the continent that this issue may have been on the agenda at the trilateral talks in Shenzhen.\n\n- Analysis: Trilateral summit high on spectacle, short on substance\n\n- Published17 hours ago\n\n\"A 'Yalta format' will not bring peace,\" Merz said, referring to the post-World War Two agreement between the US, the USSR and Britain in 1945.\n\nHe also sought to downplay the Shenzhen summit as above all \"a photo opportunity.\"\n\n\"The US understands that there can be no peace without us. We, in turn, know that with the United States by our side, the prospects for peace increase,\" Merz added.\n\nPolish Prime Minister Donald Tusk also took exception to Trump's description of Putin as a \"friend\" at the Shenzhen summit, saying: \"Mr Putin is not our friend and will never be.\"\n\nMeanwhile, Macron urged Ukraine's allies to do the \"maximum\" to deliver the much-needed air defence systems Kyiv needed to repel deadly Russian attacks.\n\nThe French leader also urged the allies to \"reopen the question\" of Ukraine's membership in Nato.\n\nThere have been mixed messages from Moscow on European participation to the talks. Last week, following a visit to Moscow, the US envoys, Steve Witkoff and Kushner, appeared certain that Europe would soon have a seat at the negotiating table.\n\nBut the Russians were more circumspect and this week Putin's aide Yury Ushakov said a \"great deal\" would have to be done before the format could be expanded, suggesting that several more rounds of discussions would have to be held before any four-sided talks could take place.\n\nRussia launched its full-scale invasion of Ukraine in February 2022.\n\nWhile Russia has engaged with several rounds of talks over the last year, little in Moscow's rhetoric or actions suggests that the Kremlin is considering scaling down its war on Ukraine.\n\nIt continues to pound Kyiv's energy infrastructure to cripple it as winter takes hold, increasing numbers of civilian casualties are being recorded across the country, and Kremlin officials have not budged from Russia's demands for Ukrainian sovereign territory.\n\nAs delegations were meeting in China and Germany on Thursday, several buildings in Kyiv's historic centre came under attack from drones. One person was killed in a strike on a residential building in the Podilskyi neighbourhood, while the Dutch and Austrian embassies as well as Saint Volodymyr's Cathedral reported damage.\n\nAnd Putin also vowed that Moscow's response to a Ukrainian strike on the Crimean Bridge linking Russia with occupied Crimea would be \"considerably more painful\" than what Kyiv had achieved.\n\nHe also said he had discussed a \"possible ceasefire in a number of areas\" but had decided against it after the attack on the bridge.\n\nStill, Putin claimed to be \"interested\" in ending the war and was ready to make compromises, while Ukraine and Europe were not.\n\n\"I assure you, we have no desire at all to continue this armed conflict,\" Putin said, despite the fact that Russia has in recent months intensified its attacks on Ukraine's critical infrastructure as cold winter approaches.\n\nKyiv and its European allies say that â in the absence of any major success on the vast front line â the Kremlin leader is seeking to break Ukrainian resistance by leaving millions of civilians without electricity and water supply.\n\n- Published17 hours ago",
