    cat: "economia-tech",
    categories: ["economia-tech"],
    title: "First-half profit falls at two out of three major Japan nonlife insurers",
    summary: "Higher U.S. interest rates and more natural disasters in the country spurred the drops, according to their earnings reports.",
    preview: "Two of Japan’s three major nonlife insurance groups logged year-on-year drops in their consolidated net profits for April-September, hit by higher U.S. interest rates and more natural disasters in the country, according to their earnings reports released on Thursday. Net profit at MS&AD Insurance Group Holdings declined 17.3% to ¥363.7 billion in the first half of the current business year, after the company recorded a substantial valuation gain a year earlier thanks to a fall in U.S. interest rates.",
    body: "Two of Japan’s three major nonlife insurance groups logged year-on-year drops in their consolidated net profits for April-September, hit by higher U.S. interest rates and more natural disasters in the country, according to their earnings reports released on Thursday.\n\nNet profit at MS&AD Insurance Group Holdings declined 17.3% to ¥363.7 billion in the first half of the current business year, after the company recorded a substantial valuation gain a year earlier thanks to a fall in U.S. interest rates.\n\nThe firm also saw increased insurance payout due to a powerful earthquake in Kumamoto Prefecture in July and a heavy rain disaster in Chiba Prefecture in August.\n\nSompo Holdings incurred a valuation loss on its bond holdings because of a surge in U.S. interest rates, logging a net profit of ¥213.8 billion, down 40.7%.\n\nIn contrast, net profit at Tokio Marine Holdings climbed 7.9% to ¥478.4 billion on the back of a decrease in large-scale fire insurance claims.\n\nInsurance premium revenue rose at all three groups, aided by premium hikes for vehicle and fire insurance products.\n\nMS&AD said Thursday that its two life insurance units — Mitsui Sumitomo Aioi Life Insurance and Mitsui Sumitomo Primary Life Insurance — will merge in October 2028 in order to beef up their product development and marketing abilities to survive the intensifying industry competition reflecting the country’s declining population.\n\nTheir combined premium revenue totaled about ¥2 trillion in fiscal 2025, ranking 10th or higher in the domestic life insurance industry.\n\nMitsui Sumitomo Insurance and Aioi Nissay Dowa Insurance, both nonlife insurance units of MS&AD, are already slated to merge in April 2027.",
